  "pages": [
    {
      "sections": [
        {
          "kind": "intro",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Each railroad's rule book names the aspects. **NORAC** (Northeast Corridor, NJ Transit, Horseshoe Curve) and **CROR** (Canada) are speed signalling: the aspect names the speed, Limited 45, Medium 30, Slow 15 mph. **GCOR** (BNSF, UP, Metrolink, Caltrain, Tri-Rail) is route signalling: \"Diverging\" means the turnout at its posted speed. LIRR and Metro-North have their own books with the same aspects. **Restricted speed**: max 20 mph (Canada 15), able to stop within half the range of vision. On the corridor the **cab signal** rules and **ACSES** enforces the limits; the **alerter** checks that you are awake."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "systems",
          "title": "Safety systems",
          "cols": 3,
          "items": [
            {
              "name": "Cab signals · ATC",
              "full": "Pulse-code cab signals with Automatic Train Control",
              "summary": "The cab display repeats the signal aspect and its speed. On a downgrade an alarm sounds: **acknowledge** within about 6 s and make a **suppression** brake application, or ATC applies a penalty brake.",
              "where": "Northeast Corridor, LIRR, Metro-North, NJ Transit, MBTA, Tri-Rail; Sherman Hill (UP, buggy display)",
              "quadrant": "**Gear lever** on the Amtrak, LIRR, Metro-North stock and the NJT Multi-Level; **ENG 2** on the MBTA cab cars and the SD70ACe. NJT locos, Arrow III, Tri-Rail locos: in the cab"
            },
            {
              "name": "ACSES II",
              "full": "Advanced Civil Speed Enforcement System",
              "summary": "Transponders give the civil speed limits and **positive stops** at home signals. The display shows the target speed; brake before the restriction. Warning: about 8 s to brake.",
              "where": "Amtrak NEC, NJ Transit, Metro-North, LIRR, MBTA",
              "quadrant": "Left aux square = ACSES · ATC fuse or cut-out"
            },
            {
              "name": "PTC (I-ETMS)",
              "full": "Positive Train Control",
              "summary": "In the real world GPS and wayside messages enforce signals, speed limits and authorities. **In TSW not enforced**: the PTC fuse only lights the display.",
              "where": "Real: BNSF, UP, Metrolink, Caltrain (I-ETMS), Tri-Rail. TSW: fuse on the F125, F59PHR, MP36, Arrow III, Tri-Rail locos",
              "quadrant": "PTC fuse on the left aux square where fitted"
            },
            {
              "name": "ATS",
              "full": "Automatic Train Stop (intermittent)",
              "summary": "An inductor at each signal showing anything but Clear sounds an alarm: **acknowledge** (forestall) within about 8 s or the brakes apply.",
              "where": "Ex-Santa Fe lines: Metrolink San Bernardino and Antelope Valley; TSW's Tri-Rail Rotem also has it",
              "quadrant": "**ENG 2** ATS ack on the Rotem cab cars; F125, F59PHR, MP36: in the cab. Left aux = ATS fuse"
            },
            {
              "name": "Alerter",
              "full": "Driver vigilance",
              "summary": "With no control moved for a while (shorter at speed), a light then a buzzer: **reset** or the train gets a penalty brake. Any control movement also resets.",
              "where": "Every modern locomotive and cab car",
              "quadrant": "**Gear lever** = alerter reset on most locos; Caltrain Bi-Level: ENG 2; F7s: safety pedal / deadman. Aux square = alerter fuse"
            },
            {
              "name": "CROR signals",
              "full": "Canadian Rail Operating Rules",
              "summary": "Canada names aspects by this and the next signal: Clear to Stop, Clear to Limited, Limited to Clear. Same lamps, different grammar (flashing green = Clear to Limited).",
              "where": "Oakville Subdivision (CN, Metrolinx)",
              "quadrant": "CN locos: gear lever = alerter"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
